NNAAMMEE
     velplot - put a vellum drawing on a page

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     vveellpplloott [-TT llpp|ppss|hhppggll] [-wwiiddee] [-ffiitt] [-ssccaallee _n] _f_i_l_e._d ...
     vveellpplloott -ttiillee [-nn] [-TT llpp|ppss|hhppggll] _b_i_g._d ...

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     vveellpplloott renders drawings made with vveelllluumm onto whatever the
     shop owns.  It writes to standard output and reads no ter-
     minal: the whole pipeline runs from mmaakkee(1), from a script,
     or down a serial line on a machine with no bitmap card at
     all.

     Each argument is a SHEET, and the file name - is standard
     input, so a generated drawing plots without a temporary
     file: vveelliinnffoo -ssyymmsshheeeett ppiidd.ssyymm | vveellpplloott -TT ppss -ffiitt - .
     Layers marked no-print, and the construction layer, are
     skipped.

     The device is chosen with -TT:

              llpp          Epson raster on stdout - ESC * 24-row head
                          bands, one grid unit = 8 dots by default.  Pipe
                          it to llpprr.  -wwiiddee prints landscape (the page
                          turned a quarter) and -ssccaallee _n picks n dots
                          per grid unit, so a small drawing prints big
              ppss          PostScript (Level 1), one page per sheet - the
                          laser's page, at a pinned 16 grid units to the
                          inch.  Text is Courier sized so the monospace
                          advance matches the editor cell: nothing collides
                          on paper that did not collide on screen.  Smooth
                          polylines emit real curveto, vertical text
                          rotates, and arcs are true arcs
              hhppggll        HPGL pen-plotter commands on stdout.  The
                          annotation layer draws in the SECOND pen (SP2) -
                          the cheapest two-color print this machine will
                          ever do

     -ffiitt scales the drawing to fill the page; for -TT llpp it picks
     the widest step of the discrete 2..32 dot ladder instead,
     because the head has no fractional dot.

TTIILLIINNGG
     -ttiillee is the sheet BIGGER than the paper.  The chosen device
     runs once per PAGE with the drawing origin stepped, so a
     drawing wider than the head comes out as pages that TAPE
     TOGETHER: one grid unit of overlap on each seam, crop marks
     at the corners and a "2/6 row B col 2" label in the margin,
     row-major so the pile is in reading order.

     It composes with -wwiiddee and -ssccaallee. It does NOT compose with
     -ffiitt, which means "make this ONE page" and is the opposite
     instruction; that combination is an error, not a precedence
     rule.  -nn prints how many pages it would take and stops.


